Move the focus into and out of the web content when navigating by keyboard.

The Edge backend asked WebView2 to move the focus with
COREWEBVIEW2_MOVE_FOCUS_REASON_PROGRAMMATIC in all cases, which focuses the web
content window without giving the focus to any element of the document and
without saying in which direction the focus is travelling. Nothing handled
ICoreWebView2Controller::MoveFocusRequested either, so the focus could never
leave the web view again: Tab moved the focus into a wxWebView and left it
there until the user clicked somewhere else.

Pass _NEXT or _PREVIOUS when the focus arrives while Tab is pressed, so that
WebView2 puts the DOM focus on the first (or, for Shift+Tab, the last)
focusable element of the page. The key state is what tells us the direction:
neither wxFocusEvent nor SetFocusFromKbd(), which is not even called when the
focus is moved by the native dialog navigation code, carries it. PROGRAMMATIC
is still used when the focus doesn't come from Tab, e.g. on a mouse click, so
nothing changes there.

Handle MoveFocusRequested, which WebView2 raises when the user tabs past the
last (or before the first) focusable element of the page, by continuing the
traversal in the window containing the control with wxWindow::Navigate(), so
that the focus leaves the web view instead of cycling inside it forever.

MoveFocus() is asynchronous, so a flag set around the call itself can't tell
the answer to our own request from a genuine request to leave the web view:
depending on the timing it either misses the answer or swallows the genuine
request, in both cases leaving the focus somewhere the user didn't ask for.
Remember the direction we asked for instead and clear it when the web content
takes the focus, which GotFocus tells us about, or when MoveFocusRequested
arrives, i.e. always from an event and never after some delay. A page which
refuses the focus is asked for it once more without a direction before giving
up on it: not all pages take the focus when they're asked to start at their
first or last focusable element, and passing the traversal on in that case
would make their content unreachable from the keyboard.

Finally, always claim the request as handled. Its default action is to look
for the next tab stop among the native child windows of the application, which
knows nothing about the order in which wx navigates its windows, and to cycle
the focus inside the page when it doesn't find one, trapping the user in the
web content. Asking the parents of the control in turn, as wxControlContainer
does when it runs out of children of its own, is a better last resort when
wxWindow::Navigate() finds nowhere to move the focus to.

